\pard\sa180\sb60\f0\fs34\b io_equilibrium_sim \u8212? gr\u8594?PC synapse drift: handoff\b0\par
\par
\pard\sa80\f0\fs21 Written 2026-09-18 for a fresh agent (Codex) picking up overnight. Everything below is established with measurements in this repo; the "Open work" section at the end is what has NOT been done.\par
\par
\pard\sa80\f0\fs21 Working dir: /home/sk57289/io_equilibrium_sim (a git repo, with uncommitted changes predating this work \u8212? see "Files" below for exactly which diffs are mine). Nothing has been committed; the user has not asked for a commit.\par
\par
\pard\sa120\sb260\f0\fs26\b 1. The standing goal\b0\par
\par
\pard\sa80\f0\fs21 Diagnose why individual gr\u8594?PC (PF\u8594?PKJ) synapses drift, WITHOUT altering core architecture.\par
\par
\pard\sa80\f0\fs21 Model context the user supplied: gr\u8594?PC synapses undergo LTD near a CF input, LTP otherwise. They are stable only if CF/IO activity sits at equilibrium (~1 Hz), which is set by the feedback loop PC \u8594? DCN (inhibitory) \u8594? IO (inhibitory) \u8594? CF \u8594? LTD at PC. That loop only regulates the POPULATION AVERAGE IO rate. Individual IO neurons can still drift because the loop is "fully closed" only when (1) an IO neuron is influenced only by DCN neurons driven by the same PCs its own CF contacts, and (2) every PC an IO neuron contacts itself modulates that same IO. Real divergence (~8-10 PCs/IO) and convergence (~45 PCs/DCN) violate both.\par
\par
\pard\sa80\f0\fs21 Gap junctions are meant to pull outliers back toward equilibrium via passive Ohmic current \u8212? NOT to synchronize spiking. Synchronized IO firing is pathological (tremor, cf. harmaline). The expected desynchroniser: variable burst size (1-3 spikes) \u8594? variable post-spike AHP (Ca-activated K+ after voltage-gated Ca2+ entry) \u8594? shuffles which sub-threshold cycle (8-10 Hz) each neuron next fires on.\par
\par
\pard\sa120\sb260\f0\fs26\b 2. Hard constraints (still binding)\b0\par
\par
\pard\fi-280\li400\sa80\f0\fs21 \bullet\tab No new or removed cell types. No changing IO\u8594?PC or PC\u8594?DCN ratios. No altering the three-neuron feedback topology.\par
\pard\fi-280\li400\sa80\f0\fs21 \bullet\tab Do not change dynamics already established as correct: gap junctions as passive Ohmic low-pass coupling; sub-threshold oscillation as timing reference; AHP as Ca-activated K+ following Ca2+ entry; LTD-then-else-LTP rule structure.\par
\pard\fi-280\li400\sa80\f0\fs21 \bullet\tab Do not retune coupling strength or window parameters as a "fix" before there is evidence of why the current config fails. Tuning is downstream of diagnosis.\par
\pard\fi-280\li400\sa80\f0\fs21 \bullet\tab Code changes limited to instrumentation, plus \u8212? once confirmed \u8212? the minimal parameter-level correction. Never a structural change.\par
\pard\fi-280\li400\sa80\f0\fs21 \bullet\tab User's added instruction, verbatim: "don't alter any rates or anything just to match the white paper, and don't diverge too far from real biology".\par
\par
\pard\sa120\sb260\f0\fs26\b 3. Verdict: the diagnosis is COMPLETE\b0\par
\par
\pard\sa80\f0\fs21 The drift is NOT caused by the olive. It is a degrees-of-freedom property of the plasticity rule's form, and it survives every manipulation of the olive.\par
\par
\pard\sa80\f0\fs21 Variance decomposition by level (synapse < Purkinje cell < CF territory), 600 s, 2 seeds:\par
\par
\pard\f1\fs17   condition               within PKJ   between PKJ   between CF   var frac within\par
\pard\f1\fs17   baseline                  0.2439        0.0106       0.0050         99.8%\par
\pard\f1\fs17   gap junctions off         0.2387        0.0104       0.1076         83.0%\par
\pard\f1\fs17   gap_g 0.04 (strong)       0.2573        0.0109       0.0045         99.8%\par
\pard\f1\fs17   IO heterogeneity cv 0.15  0.2387        0.0107       0.0037         99.8%\par
\pard\f1\fs17   DCN\u8594?IO misrouted          0.2431        0.0102       0.0045         99.8%\par
\par
\pard\sa80\f0\fs21 The within-cell column moves 7% across manipulations that swing the between-territory column 24x. 99.8% of all weight variance is inside single Purkinje cells.\par
\par
\pard\sa80\f0\fs21 Driving the shipped Plasticity object with NO olive, NO coupling, NO loop, and a CF pinned at exactly 1 Hz reproduces the entire spread: 0.2308 @350 s \u8594? 0.2890 @1800 s \u8594? 0.2873 @3600 s, converging on 1/sqrt(12) = 0.2887, the SD of the uniform distribution on [0,1], with the mean flat at 0.507. Single-synapse autocorrelation from the earlier 5-hour campaign: tau = 537 s.\par
\par
\pard\sa80\f0\fs21 Why: the rule is additive, so step size ignores w \u8212? no per-synapse fixed point exists. All 500 synapses on a cell resolve against one shared CF verdict, so the loop's restoring force is common-mode and cancels out of their differences. And the loop's only sensor is PKJ rate, a function of sum(w), so any redistribution preserving the sum is invisible: 40 CF territories regulating 160,000 weights. The between-PKJ column is not independently regulated either \u8212? 0.0106 is just the within-cell walk over sqrt(500) (0.2439/sqrt(500) = 0.0109).\par
\par
\pard\sa120\sb260\f0\fs26\b 4. The five candidates, in the order the goal specified\b0\par
\par
\pard\fi-280\li400\sa80\f0\fs21 1.\tab Coupling conductance too weak \u8212? RULED OUT. I_gap RMS 0.358 uA/cm2 vs I_leak RMS 0.698, about half the leak.\par
\par
\pard\fi-280\li400\sa80\f0\fs21 2.\tab Coupling synchronizing instead of equilibrating \u8212? CONFIRMED. This is a real departure from the paper. With coupling on, all 40 IO cells emit IDENTICAL CF counts: 609/609 (gap_g 0.0143), 603/603 (0.04), 608/608 (het cv 0.15), and 1821/1821 over 1800 s. Per-cell CF rate SD is exactly 0.0000; min = max. The k-th volley of every cell lands within 78 ms (53 ms at strong coupling). That is 1:1 entrainment; a restoring force would leave residual scatter. Gap off: counts 595-625, rate SD 0.0273, range 0.947-1.063 Hz, 91 ms pairwise jitter, CF synchrony index 0.052 vs 0.234. Nearest-neighbour Vm correlation 0.83, floor ~0.11 at max ring distance. In the one run where dispersion survives, corr(I_gap, rate deviation) is -0.316 / -0.546 \u8212? so the current's SIGN is right; in the shipped config that correlation is undefined (nan) because the predictor has zero variance, not because of a code bug.\par
\par
\pard\fi-280\li400\sa80\f0\fs21 3.\tab Burst-dependent AHP missing/miscalibrated \u8212? CONFIRMED ABSENT, and this is why (2) happens. Zero ISIs under 100 ms in every condition (CF events are single Ca2+ spikes; the MVP spec excluded multi-spike bursts). Peak [Ca]i CV = 0.0052, so corr(Ca peak, next ISI) = +0.02. Nothing shuffles phase, so coupling meets no opposition. Two contributing conditions: the dominant sub-threshold rhythm peaks at 2.4 Hz, not 8-10 Hz (consistent with the 3.00 Hz intrinsic rate), giving only ~3 cycles per inter-CF interval to shuffle between instead of ~10; and the one available desynchroniser, noise_sigma, breaks the loop (see section 7).\par
\par
\pard\fi-280\li400\sa80\f0\fs21 4.\tab Feedback-loop closure violations \u8212? RULED OUT for this effect. Misrouting DCN\u8594?IO changes within-PKJ 0.2431 vs 0.2439 and between-IO 0.0045 vs 0.0050.\par
\par
\pard\fi-280\li400\sa80\f0\fs21 5.\tab delta+/delta- or LTD-window imbalance \u8212? NOT PRESENT; delta MAGNITUDE is the operative parameter. Mean weight is flat (0.34-0.38 over 1800 s; -0.001/hour over 5 hours), so the ratio balances correctly. What delta- = 0.009 sets is the diffusion rate.\par
\par
\pard\sa120\sb260\f0\fs26\b 5. The user's per-synapse-CF question \u8212? answered, tested\b0\par
\par
\pard\sa80\f0\fs21 "Would making it so that the climbing fibers affect individual synapses instead of individual Purkinje cells help the drifting?" No. Shared CF gives 0.2888 @1800 s; one CF per synapse gives 0.2883. Identical. Splitting the CF adds a noise source; the shared verdict was never what contributed. (A per-synapse POISSON CF read 0.2165, but with mean w 0.770 \u8212? ceiling pileup, because Poisson gives P(LTD) = 1-exp(-0.1) = 0.0952 instead of 0.100, which unbalances the ratio. That is higher diffusion, not lower.)\par
\par
