\pard\sa80\f0\fs21 Baseline run: rate 1.0078 Hz, counts 1821-1821, sync 0.232, Vm corr 0.249, nn 0.832. wd1 run:      rate 1.3656 Hz, counts 2476-2476, sync 0.245, Vm corr 0.230, nn 0.839.\par
\par
\pard\sa80\f0\fs21 Option A \u8212? scale both delta, hold the ratio at 9. Strictly parameter-level. Preserves the equilibrium rate to four digits, the operating point, H2's ratio prediction, and PF-pool- size rate invariance. Spread scales with delta (0.0666 measured vs 0.070 predicted unbounded), mean w 0.357. It DELAYS rather than cures: diffusion goes as delta^2, so filling [0,1] moves from ~500 s to ~12,500 s. Independent biological support: delta- = 0.009 is ~13x in-vitro PF-PC LTD (~40% over ~300 pairings ~= 0.0007/pairing). This is the recommended lead option.\par
\par
\pard\sa80\f0\fs21 Option B \u8212? weight_dependence = 1.0. The only setting that makes the spread STATIONARY, an 11x reduction, with no synchrony penalty. Costs: the balance condition picks up a (1-w)/w factor, moving equilibrium CF rate to 1.3656 Hz (predicted 1.37 from delta-/delta+ = (P_LTP/P_LTD)(1-w)/w at w = 0.421). The bigger cost is that it COUPLES rate to weight: under the additive rule delta-/delta+ sets the rate and weight is free, which is exactly why the earlier PF-pool sweep found rate invariant with weight scaling as 1/n_PF. With soft bounds, changing pool size would move the rate instead. Treat it as a different model, not a tuning. NOTE: the user said not to alter rates to match the white paper \u8212? B moves a rate, so it must not be adopted as default without the user saying so. It is deliberately left at 0.0 in config.\par
\par
\pard\sa120\sb260\f0\fs26\b 7. Known confounds and traps \u8212? read before re-running anything\b0\par
\par
\pard\fi-280\li400\sa80\f0\fs21 \bullet\tab noise_sigma = 2.0 BREAKS THE LOOP. CF rate goes to 2.99 Hz (free-running), mean w 0.0032, 39% of synapses pinned. Root cause: dcn_io_gaba_gain = 0.0974 was fitted at noise_sigma = 0, per config's own comment. A clean membrane-noise test REQUIRES re-running experiments/run_calibration.py with noise on first. The "ionoise" conditions in ~/drift_diag are therefore inconclusive and confounded \u8212? do not cite them as results, only as a positive control for corr(I_gap, rate deviation).\par
\pard\fi-280\li400\sa80\f0\fs21 \bullet\tab The isolated Monte-Carlo null-window row reads SD 0.018, which LOOKS like stability but is bound pileup: mean w 0.028, i.e. weights crashed to the floor. With a CF at 1 Hz and a 200 ms null window, delta-/delta+ = 9 no longer balances (needs 7). Always check the MEAN alongside the SD.\par
\pard\fi-280\li400\sa80\f0\fs21 \bullet\tab corr(I_gap, rate deviation) = nan in coupled conditions is correct behaviour, not a bug: the predictor has identically zero variance because of the 1:1 entrainment.\par
\pard\fi-280\li400\sa80\f0\fs21 \bullet\tab /home/sk57289/longsim NO LONGER EXISTS. The 5-hour campaign's raw arrays are gone; only summary.json and the PNGs survive. A memory file claiming otherwise has been corrected.\par
\pard\fi-280\li400\sa80\f0\fs21 \bullet\tab pytest is NOT installed. Use: python3 -m unittest discover -s tests -q (97 tests, ~500 s, OK both before and after all changes).\par
\pard\fi-280\li400\sa80\f0\fs21 \bullet\tab The box is bandwidth-bound: keep simulation concurrency near 10, not 64.\par
\pard\fi-280\li400\sa80\f0\fs21 \bullet\tab cf_times is saved as a ragged object array; prefer ev_io / ev_t for analysis.\par
\pard\fi-280\li400\sa80\f0\fs21 \bullet\tab To wait on background runs, use a blocking loop (until <cond>; do sleep N; done) and wait for the task-completion notification. Repeated short polling wastes turns because wall-clock advances only seconds between turns.\par
